{"id":"2ab4c4e1-62e2-4fc8-99a1-517ab2f0d8f1","object":"chat.completion","created":1785035766,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"threshold\\\" not in case or \\\"calls\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'threshold' and 'calls'\\\")\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        required_fields = [\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"loss_without_call\\\", \\\"loss_with_call\\\", \\\"prediction_tokens\\\", \\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        for field in required_fields:\\n            if field not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"call missing required field '{field}'\\\")\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        prediction_tokens = call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        if prediction_tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive\\\")\\n        normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\\n        normalized_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\\n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain_rounded >= threshold)\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted,\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain_rounded\\n        })\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that processes a case payload containing threshold and calls. For each call, compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Then normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (since \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token\" means divide by prediction_tokens, and note that prediction_tokens is integer >=1? but we must handle possible 0? The spec says \"at least one prediction token\", so we assume prediction_tokens >=1). Accepted if parseable is True and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nReturn accepted_call_ids list of strings, and diagnostics list of dicts with accepted (bool), call_id, normalized_gain (number - should be rounded? The description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". In the output schema, normalized_gain is number, not integer, but likely we should round to some precision? The fixture data has floats with many decimals. The contract doesn't specify rounding. The instruction \"Return ... rounded per-call diagnostics.\" suggests we should round the normalized_gain. Probably to a reasonable number of decimal places, maybe 8? But the output schema just says number. I think we can compute and leave as float with full precision, but the phrase \"rounded\" might imply rounding. However, looking at typical implementations for such tasks, they often round to 8 decimal places. But the schema only says number, not integer. The example fixtures don't show expected outputs. The instruction: \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So we should round normalized_gain. I'll round to 8 decimal places, or maybe use Python's round to avoid floating point issues. But to be safe, we can round to 8 decimals because that is common. However, the JSON serialization will produce the number as is; rounding to a specific number of digits is not required by the schema. But the instruction says \"rounded\". I'll implement rounding to 8 decimal places using round(x, 8).\n\nThe output must have accepted_call_ids list and diagnostics list in the same order as calls (keep diagnostics in supplied call order). The diagnostics must contain each call, even those not accepted? The schema items have required \"accepted\" bool, so we need to include all calls in the diagnostics list. That matches \"keep diagnostics in supplied call order\" meaning for each call.\n\nAlso note: \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we only use threshold, calls array, and each call's fields.\n\nImplementation: iterate over calls, compute normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens. Check if parseable is True and normalized_gain >= threshold. Then build accepted_call_ids and diagnostics list.\n\nReturn dict with keys \"accepted_call_ids\" and \"diagnostics\".\n\nEdge cases: If case missing fields, raise ValueError. The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". We'll do basic validation: check that case has \"threshold\" and \"calls\", each call has required fields.\n\nAlso note: The case payload may have \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nImplementation code must be a string within JSON.\n\nThus final answer: JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing Python source code.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if \"threshold\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"case must contain 'threshold' and 'calls'\")\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    \n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        # Validate required fields in each call\n        required_fields = [\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"]\n        for field in required_fields:\n            if field not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"call missing required field '{field}'\")\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        prediction_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        # compute normalized gain\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # prevent division by zero? prediction_tokens should be at least 1 per spec, but check\n        if prediction_tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be positive\")\n        normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\n        # round to 8 decimal places\n        normalized_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\n        \n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain_rounded >= threshold)\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain_rounded\n        })\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nThat's the core. Ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nNow we need to embed as a JSON string. The string must be escaped properly. We'll produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the string of Python code.\n\nWe'll output exactly that.\n\nMake sure no extra text."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2164,"completion_tokens":1566,"total_tokens":3730,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1148},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":116},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}